## Thought
Most of the task is plain. The terms that could be read more than one way are these:

- "Class 8 tractors" is probably the standard US heavy-duty truck class (over 33,000 lb GVWR), but "tractor" could also mean a day-cab or sleeper tractor. The cab type affects battery size and price.
- "Federal and Indiana incentives that actually apply to a purchase in 2026" is ambiguous because the federal Section 45W commercial clean vehicle credit was set to end for vehicles acquired after Sept 30, 2025. The EPA Clean Heavy-Duty Vehicles grants and HVIP-style state programs are other candidates. "Indiana incentives" could mean state grants, utility programs, or the VW settlement funds.
- "Indiana commercial electricity rate" could mean the average commercial retail rate (EIA), a specific utility tariff (e.g. Duke Energy Indiana, AES Indiana, NIPSCO, I&M), or a demand-charge-inclusive EV rate. "Indiana diesel price" could mean the EIA Midwest on-highway average, the AAA Indiana average, or a bulk fleet price net of tax.
- "Charging infrastructure" could mean depot Level 2 or DC fast chargers (e.g. 150 kW), or public charging. The scope could also include make-ready and utility upgrades.
- "7-year total cost of ownership" is ambiguous on discounting and on whether to include residual value.
- "Word (.docx) memo": the format is clear.
- "Regional routes under 250 miles a day" is clear, but it affects whether battery range is sufficient.

